# Grimoire Domain Registry & Pseudo-URL Routing (DSAP evolution)

<rule>
name: grimoire_domain_routing_standard
description: Prescriptive contract for all pseudo-URL routing in the Grimoire browser. Domain name (primary + aliases) resolves to registered applet/handler. Centralized in registry. Modal + panes + desktop shortcuts are thin routers. No per-domain if-chains outside registry files. Covers client dsapRegistry + grimoireCoreDomains, server grimoireDomainRegistry, activate() for core surfaces, hosted/shim applets, host bridge, lifecycle, standalone, and WS packet ownership by domain.
filters:
  - type: content
    pattern: "(?i)(dsap|registerDsap|grimoireDomain|grimoireCoreDomains|resolveDsap|navigateDsap|edtx://|rdf://|wiki\\.danbooru|en\\.grimoire\\.jp|pseudo.*(url|browser|domain)|grimoire.*router)"
  - type: file_extension
    pattern: "\\.(js|html|mdc)$"
actions:
  - type: suggest
    message: |
      Follow .cursor/rules/dsap-applet-creation.mdc (Grimoire Domain Routing):
      - All routing decisions live in the registry (client: dsapRegistry.js + grimoireCoreDomains.js; server: modules/grimoireDomainRegistry.js).
      - Register domains by primary `url`/`domain` + `aliases`. Use `activate(shell, match)` for core surfaces that drive shell methods (wiki pages, search, home). Pure applets use getContent+drivers or shimActivate.
      - Never add string-matching if-chains (lower.includes('en.grimoire.jp'), /wiki.danbooru/, etc.) for routing in tagWikiSearchModal.js, desktopShortcuts.js, or pane logic. Delegate to resolve + navigateDsapIfMatched (or equivalent).
      - Core built-ins (en.grimoire.jp family, wiki.danbooru.jp, docs.novelai.jp, etc.) are registered domains, not special cases.
      - Server domains can own WS packets via `packets` map or registerPacketHandler (delegation in websocketHandlers routeMessage).
      - Manifests in dsapManifests.js or grimoireCoreDomains.js; impls self-register.
metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 3.0
</rule>

## New Unified Design (Grimoire Domain Routing)

**Core principle**: A domain name (primary + aliases) resolves to a registered applet or handler function. This makes routing robust, centralised, and extensible. The main Grimoire modal, panes, and desktop shortcuts act as a **thin router + chrome** — they delegate resolution and activation; they do **not** contain hard-coded string matching for routes.

### Client side
- Primary registry: `public/scripts/comp/dsapRegistry.js` (exports `registerDsap`, `resolveDsap`, `navigateDsapIfMatched`, `grimoireStripPseudoProtocol`, `isGrimoirePseudoBrowserAddress`, host bridge, etc.).
- Core built-in surfaces are registered as domains in `public/scripts/comp/grimoireCoreDomains.js` (loaded after dsapManifests in app.html). Examples:
  - `en.grimoire.jp` (+ /search, /index, /home) — search page or home.
  - `wiki.danbooru.jp` / `wiki.e621.com` (and /tag/ forms) — direct tag wiki via `getTagWikiPageDirectly`.
  - `docs.novelai.jp` — static docs via `openStaticWikiPage` / site index.
- Registration uses the same `registerDsap({ url, aliases, activate?, getContent?, shimActivate?, ... })`.
  - `activate(shell, match)` — full control for core surfaces that need to drive shell methods (history shape, search state, etc.). The shell (TagWikiSearchModal or WikiWindowInstance) is passed so the domain can call `shell.getTagWikiPageDirectly(...)`, `shell.showSearchResultsPage(...)`, etc.
  - Pure hosted applets continue to use `getContent(match)` + `drivers { init, destroy, refresh }`.
  - Shims use `shimActivate`.
- **Routing contract**: `navigate(pseudoUrl)` (and `_navigateToPane`) must resolve first via the registry and delegate to `navigateDsapIfMatched`. **No if (lower.includes('en.grimoire.jp/search') ... ) chains** in tagWikiSearchModal.js, desktopShortcuts.js, or pane restore logic outside the registry/core domains files.

### Server side
- `modules/grimoireDomainRegistry.js` mirrors the concept:
  - `registerDomain({ domain, aliases, packets?, resolve?, getPage? })`
  - `resolvePseudoUrl(raw)` — single source of truth for `edtx://...`, bare hosts, etc.
  - WS packet ownership: domains declare `packets: { 'my_packet': 'handleMethod' }` (or use `registerPacketHandler`). Central `routeMessage` in websocketHandlers delegates before/after the big switch.
  - Future: `getPage(match)` can return pre-built HTML shells for the "server sends back prebuilt pages" goal.
- Wired into globalResources and websocketHandlers (binds packets on construction, `resolve_grimoire_url` WS message for client-driven resolution).

### Why the change
- Old design had brittle ad-hoc string matching scattered across navigate, _navigateToPane, history restore, address bar, desktop handlers, etc.
- New design: single resolution point per side. Adding a new surface (or alias/redirect) = add a registration. Core surfaces (search, home, danbooru wikis, static docs) are now first-class domains.
- Enables clean server pre-built pages + domain-owned WS packets while keeping DSAP applets fully dynamic and client-hosted.

Lightweight manifest stubs for dynamic applets live in `public/scripts/comp/dsapManifests.js`. Core built-ins are declared in `grimoireCoreDomains.js`. Both are loaded early (after dsapRegistry.js).

## Registration Patterns

### Core built-in surfaces (grimoireCoreDomains.js + registry)
Core Grimoire experiences (home, search, danbooru/e621 tag wikis, NovelAI docs, etc.) are registered as domains. They often use `activate(shell, match)` because they drive rich shell state (search filters, history shapes, direct calls to `getTagWikiPageDirectly`, `showSearchResultsPage`, `openStaticWikiPage`, etc.).

Example pattern (see grimoireCoreDomains.js):

```js
registerDsap({
  url: 'en.grimoire.jp',
  aliases: ['en.grimoire.jp/search', 'en.grimoire.jp/index', 'grimoire.jp', ...],
  title: 'Grimoire',
  type: 'core',
  activate(shell, match) {
    const norm = normalize... (match...);
    if (norm.includes('/search')) {
      // parse q, set address, call shell.showSearchResultsPage + performSearch, addToHistory with search shape
      return true;
    }
    if (...) { /* home */ }
    // ...
  }
});
```

`wiki.danbooru.jp`, `docs.novelai.jp` etc. follow the same pattern (some delegate to `shell.getTagWikiPageDirectly(tag)`).

**Rule**: All resolution for these hosts must go through the registry. The implementations in grimoireCoreDomains.js are the single place that knows the mapping.

### Dynamic / applet surfaces (dsapManifests.js + *DsapApplet.js)
For true mini-apps (quips, future ones):

- Manifest stub in `dsapManifests.js` (url + aliases + assets + toolbox). No getContent.
- Implementation file self-registers on load with `getContent(match) + drivers` (hosted) or `shimActivate` (legacy modal).

The two-file split and lazy asset rules below still apply to these.

**Do not** put core surface logic or if-chains for en.grimoire.jp/wiki.* into applet files. Core surfaces live in grimoireCoreDomains (or dedicated core domain files).

## Lazy load & unload (applet assets)

- **Scripts** in `assets.scripts` inject `<script data-dsap-asset="…">` on first visit (core domains rarely need this).
- **External CSS** in `assets.styles` inject `<link data-dsap-entry="…">` into `<head>`.
- **Inline CSS** from `getContent().css` is a `<style>` inside the shell display area.
- On navigate away (last shell hosting that entry): external styles removed, `destroy()` called, optional `onScopeExit()` on entry.
- Intra-domain route changes (same entry) keep asset scope.
- `assets.unloadScripts: true` removes script tags and clears implementation (rare).

## Domain / Applet kinds

### Core built-in domains (registered in grimoireCoreDomains.js)
Use `activate(shell, match)` when the surface needs to drive shell state (search UI, direct wiki fetch, static doc loading, address bar protocol switching, history entry shapes of type 'search'/'wiki'/'static-wiki-page', etc.).

These are loaded at startup and participate in the same resolution as applets. They are the reason the modal can be a thin router.

### Hosted applets (in-Grimoire content)
Provide `getContent(match)` returning `{ html, css?, drivers? }`.
- Root element **must** have `data-dsap="unique-id"`.
- Drivers: `init(host)` required; `destroy(host)` required; `refresh(host)` optional.

### Shim (legacy external modal)
Provide `shimActivate(shell, match)`.
- Still useful for things that open their own full modal (e.g. NAX Vibes).

## Host bridge (`host` in `init` / `refresh` / `activate`)

Use only documented members (same contract for core domains and applets):

- `host.getRoot()` — container
- `host.navigate(pseudoUrl)` — route (inside shell or globally)
- `host.refresh()`
- `host.getPathSegments()` / `host.getQueryParam(name)`
- `host.showToast(...)`
- `host.on(eventName, handler)` — ws listener (auto-cleaned)
- `host.getWsClient()`
- `host.shell` — the hosting TagWikiSearchModal / WikiWindowInstance / pane

**Do not** mutate global UI outside the host. Never call `host.setTitle()` (shell owns address bar).

## Standalone windows

- `openDsapInStandaloneWindow(url)` (or equivalent for core domains) → `wikiWindowManager.createWindow(...)` or `createDsapWindow`.
- `WikiWindowInstance` (and main modal panes) implement `navigate(url)` and delegate to the registry.
- History entries for domains use appropriate `type` ('dsap', 'wiki', 'search', 'static-wiki-page', ...).
- "Open in new window" from a domain page copies history when possible.

## Lifecycle rules

- `init(host)` / `activate` runs on navigation to the URL.
- `destroy(host)` on leaving the domain (or closing standalone).
- No background work unless explicitly started by user and torn down in destroy.
- Use custom-dropdown (see dropdown-setup.mdc) for settings inside domains/applets.

## Styling

### DSAP-SMF (retro Linksys admin applets)

Dreamscape DSAP applets (`*.dyna.dreamscape.jp`, `ispy.dreamscape.jp`, `dreamscape.jp`) use **DSAP-SMF**. See `.cursor/rules/dsap-smf-design.mdc`.

- Shared CSS: `public/css/dsap-smf.css` (load globally or `theme: 'dsap-smf'` on registration)
- Markup helpers: `public/scripts/comp/dsapSmfMarkup.js`
- Root: `class="dsap-root dsap-smf"` + `data-dsap="…"`
- Header: branch title (left) + tool name (right) via `dsapSmfBuildHeader()`
- Tab bar: table-based via `dsapSmfBuildTabBar()`; wire with `dsapSmfWireTabBar()`
- Applet-specific CSS only in `getContent().css` — do not duplicate SMF chrome rules

### General

- Scope: `[data-dsap="my-id"] …` (core domains may use other stable markers if needed).
- Prefer glass theme tokens for non-DSAP surfaces.
- Never leak to global modal / body / .btn.

## app.html script order (critical)

```html
<script src="scripts/comp/dsapRegistry.js"></script>
<script src="scripts/comp/dsapSmfMarkup.js"></script>
<script src="scripts/comp/dsapManifests.js"></script>
<script src="scripts/comp/grimoireCoreDomains.js"></script>
<script src="scripts/comp/dreamscapeHomeDsap.js"></script>
<!-- Lazy *DsapApplet.js files are NEVER listed here -->
```

Only always-needed shims may stay in app.html.

## Routing discipline (enforced)

- `navigate(pseudoUrl)` and pane equivalents **must** do registry resolution first.
- All new pseudo-hosts (or new aliases for existing ones) are added by editing the appropriate registration file (grimoireCoreDomains.js for built-ins, dsapManifests.js + impl for applets).
- Server changes for new domains/packets go in modules/grimoireDomainRegistry.js + wiring in websocketHandlers.
- Forbidden: adding more `lower.includes('en.grimoire.jp/...')`, regex on /wiki.danbooru/, special cases in desktopShortcuts handleWikiPageClick (beyond calling the registry-backed openStandalone...), history restore branches, etc.

## Checklist for new domains / applets

1. **Core surface?** Add registration (with `activate` if it needs to drive shell) to `grimoireCoreDomains.js`. Update any affected shell methods to be callable from activate.
2. **Dynamic applet?** Add manifest stub to `dsapManifests.js` (url/aliases/assets/toolbox). Create `*DsapApplet.js` that only merges `getContent` + drivers (or shimActivate).
3. Server side (if the domain owns packets or will provide prebuilt pages): `registerDomain` in a module that runs at startup, declare `packets`.
4. Ensure the thin-router contract: the new thing is reachable via address bar, toolbox (if appropriate), internal links (via _interceptAllLinks), back/forward, standalone, and desktop shortcuts.
5. Hard-refresh + service worker notify after client changes.
6. Test the full matrix: main modal, split panes, standalone window, desktop shortcut launch, address bar direct entry, history, refresh.
7. Update this rule file (version bump) if you introduce new host APIs or registration patterns.

## Reference implementations

- **Core domain (activate pattern):** grimoireCoreDomains.js (en.grimoire.jp, wiki.danbooru.jp, docs.novelai.jp).
- **Hosted + lazy applet:** Dynamic Quips (`dsapManifests.js` + `quipsDsapApplet.js`).
- **Shim:** NAX Vibes (`naxVibesApplet.js` using `shimActivate`).
- **Server registry + packet ownership:** modules/grimoireDomainRegistry.js + usage in websocketHandlers.

## Server / WS notes (for full routing picture)

- Client navigation can (and should) be able to call `resolve_grimoire_url` for server-driven decisions or prebuilt HTML.
- When a domain registers `packets`, the central routeMessage delegates so new features don't bloat the giant switch.
- This is the foundation for "server sends back prebuilt pages" while still allowing fully dynamic client applets.

Update this rule whenever the registry API, host contract, or "thin router" contract changes.
